    "body": "Simtra BioPharma Solutions has opened Building H, a more than $250 million expansion at its Bloomington, Ind., campus that gives the company more room to make sterile injectable drugs.\nWhat’s Related\nThe contract manufacturer held a ribbon-cutting on Monday for the 153,000-square-foot building. Indiana Gov. Mike Braun, Bloomington Mayor Kerry Thomson, and other local officials joined company leaders. Construction started in June 2024.\nBuilding H adds prefilled syringe filling, vial filling, and lyophilization, the freeze-drying step many injectable drugs need to stay stable. WFIU reported that the building was designed for high-speed isolator syringe filling lines and several large freeze dryers.\n“Building H represents much more than additional manufacturing capacity. It is an important milestone in the growth strategy we are executing across Simtra,” said Franco Negron, Simtra’s chief executive officer. “We are investing in the capacity, technology and infrastructure our customers need to develop and manufacture increasingly complex sterile injectable medicines.”\nPart of a bigger build-out\nBuilding H is one piece of a larger plan. Simtra has also invested more than $125 million in added space at its Halle, Germany, site and an initial $150 million-plus in its new Bloomington Park Campus. All told, the company plans to grow from 11 filling lines to 18 by 2027 across its U.S. and European network.\n“Our customers are planning years ahead, and they need manufacturing partners that can do the same,” said Ray Guidotti, chief operating officer. “Building H gives us significant new capacity in Bloomington today to go along with our expansion in Halle, while investment in our new Park campus creates additional options for the future. Together, these investments give customers greater flexibility in where and how they manufacture and provide a foundation that can scale as their programs grow.”\nFor drugmakers , that means more choice about where to place a product and more room to ramp up as a drug moves from clinical trials to commercial volumes.\nA boost for Bloomington\nSimtra says the expansion is expected to create “hundreds of high-quality jobs ” in Bloomington. WFIU reported that the company has created 450 jobs so far.\n“You’re looking at 250 million dollars, hundreds of jobs and you’re investing a quarter of a billion,” Braun said at the event, according to WFIU. “That says a lot, so you’re lucky to be the community that’s getting the benefit of it.”\nThomson pointed to the reach of what gets made there. “People who live and work in Bloomington are helping produce therapies that can make an enormous difference in the lives of people they will never meet,” she said.\n“Opening Building H is an important achievement for our Bloomington team and for everyone who has helped bring this expansion from concept to reality,” said Pat Adams, vice president of expansion operations. “It also creates a strong foundation for the continued growth of our Bloomington operations.”",
